.he "group (1)                    9/30/26                    group (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
group
- file group
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
group [ -newgroup ] file ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Every Micronix file has associated with it a group number, used to
determine group access permission.  Group reports that number, and
with an option changes it.
.sp
With no option, group reports the group of each named file.  The
number is looked up in /etc/group and printed as a group name where
there is one, and as a number where there is not.
.sp
With -newgroup, group changes the group of each named file.  Newgroup
may be a decimal group number or a group name looked up in
/etc/group.
.sp
Only the super-user may change the group of a file; anyone may ask
what group one belongs to.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-newgroup	Change the group to newgroup instead of reporting it.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
This is the group-side twin of owner (1): the same two modes, the
same argument scan, and the same report.  The name is looked up with
getgrnam and the number with getgrgid, both in libc - see
getgrent (3).
.sp
Chown here sets an owner and a group at once, the pair packed as
uid | gid << 8, and has no -1 to leave a field alone.  So every change
stats the file first and carries its owner through unchanged.
.sp
A group name need not exist for a number to be used: a decimal
argument is taken as itself and /etc/group is not consulted.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/etc/group - group name and number relationships
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
chgrp (1), owner (1), chmod (1), ls (1)
